@page "/docs/common/session-recovery"

@* Session recovery: an interactive server session (circuit) holds the page in server memory and
   ends with a lost connection, a restart or memory pressure. Pausing and resuming it: what IzzyBlazor components keep,
   what the app persists, the reconnect dialog, and .NET 11's pause in a hidden tab. Each claim
   was checked on .NET 11 (Nov 10, 2026) with Blazor.pauseCircuit/resumeCircuit and AddAutoPause. *@

<ArticlePage Section="common" Slug="session-recovery">
    <Lead>
        An interactive server page runs in a session on the server. The session ends when the connection drops, the server
        restarts or the server runs short of memory, and the user's work is lost with it. Blazor can pause the session
        instead and resume it later. The resumed page restores what's in the URL or marked <Code>[PersistentState]</Code>.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <TextBlock>
            A pause saves the page's state in the browser and frees the server's memory. Resuming starts a new session from
            that state and rebuilds every component.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What comes back</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Table and DataGrid sorting, filters, search and page.</strong> They're stored in the URL, so a resumed
                page shows the same rows. When you bind a grid, such as with <Code>@@bind-Page</Code>, the grid restores only
                what your property restores.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Anything you bind and mark <Code>[PersistentState]</Code>.</strong> The components rebuild from it. A
                Dialog bound with <Code>@@bind-Open</Code> opens again. A TextField or RichTextEditor shows its text again,
                and a grid shows its page.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                <strong>Nothing you leave unmarked.</strong> Bound values without <Code>[PersistentState]</Code> reset, as
                after a reload. A file chosen in a FileUpload is also lost, because the browser can't restore it.
            </ListItem>
        </List>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Orders.razor" HighlightLines="13,14,15">@Persisted</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A persisted value must serialize to JSON. Persist only what the user would miss: what they typed, chose or opened.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">The reconnect dialog</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            When <Code>App.razor</Code> contains a <Anchor Href="docs/components/reconnect-dialog">ReconnectDialog</Anchor>,
            a paused session shows "Session paused" and a Resume button. Resume starts the new session. The message states
            whether the server or the page paused the session. When a session can't resume, the dialog offers Reload.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Pause a hidden tab <Badge Accent="Accent.Primary" Size="BadgeSize.Sm">.NET 11+</Badge></Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            .NET 11 can pause a session on its own after its tab has been hidden for a set time, and resume it when the user
            returns to the tab. Add the <Code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</Code> package, then turn it on where
            the app maps its components:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="csharp" Title="Program.cs">@AutoPause</Code>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>
                The session doesn't pause while a text field or a RichTextEditor has focus, so text the user is still typing
                isn't lost.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>
                On phones, the browser stops scripts on a background page within seconds, often before the delay runs out.
                The session then ends as a lost connection, and the user gets it back through the reconnect dialog.
            </ListItem>
            <ListItem>When the user returns to a paused tab, it resumes on its own. The dialog shows only while it resumes.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Pause from your own code</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            To pause on a rule of your own, such as after a time without input, call <Code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</Code> and
            <Code>Blazor.resumeCircuit()</Code> from the page's script. On .NET 11, the server can also ask a page to pause,
            such as before a deployment or restart. See Microsoft's
            <Anchor Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/state-management/server#pause-and-resume-circuits" External>pause and resume circuits</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">Render modes</Anchor>: what components do on static and interactive pages.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/reconnect-dialog">ReconnectDialog</Anchor>: the dialog's states and text.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private const string Persisted = """
        @page "/orders"
        @rendermode InteractiveServer

        <DataGrid Items="Orders" @bind-Page="Page" PageSize="20">
            ...
        </DataGrid>

        <Dialog Title="Notes" @bind-Open="NotesOpen">
            <RichTextEditor Label="Notes" @bind-Value="Notes" />
        </Dialog>

        @code {
            [PersistentState] public int Page { get; set; } = 1;
            [PersistentState] public bool NotesOpen { get; set; }
            [PersistentState] public string? Notes { get; set; }
        }
        """;

    private const string AutoPause = """
        using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;   // AddAutoPause

        app.MapRazorComponents<App>()
            .AddInteractiveServerRenderMode()
            .WithBrowserOptions(options =>
                options.AddAutoPause(pause => pause.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2)));
        """;
}
